---
import DisclosureIcon from "../DisclosureIcon.astro";
import ChapterFrame from "../ChapterFrame.astro";
import PolicyComparison from "../PolicyComparison.astro";
import QuestionSequence from "../renewal/QuestionSequence.astro";
import { defenseOptions } from "../../data/scenarios.js";
---

<ChapterFrame id="defense">
  <Fragment slot="heading"
    ><h2 id="defense-heading">
      The same capability.<br /><em>Whose advantage?</em>
    </h2><p>
      A system that finds a weakness can help someone repair it or exploit it.
      Restricting access can reduce exposure while withholding tools from people
      who need to defend themselves.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
  <QuestionSequence kind="defense"
    ><article data-question>
      <div class="scenario-question">
        <h3>
          A small maintainer protects<br />a very large dependency.
        </h3><p>
          In this illustrative situation, a new model can identify serious
          vulnerabilities. An under-resourced maintainer wants to use it on
          software relied on by thousands of organisations. How should access
          work?
        </p>
      </div>
      <PolicyComparison
        id="defense-access"
        legend="Who can use the capability?"
        options={defenseOptions}
      />
    </article></QuestionSequence
  >
  <p class="scenario-note">
    These are compatible policy ingredients, not exhaustive alternatives. A
    supported programme can use vetting and later broaden access. Outcomes
    depend on implementation; these comparisons are not measured forecasts.
  </p>
  <details class="evidence">
    <summary
      ><span>Ground this in the real world</span><span class="summary-hint"
        >Mythos Preview & Project Glasswing</span
      ><DisclosureIcon /></summary
    ><div class="evidence-content">
      <h3>Finding a flaw<br />starts another obligation.</h3><div>
        <p>
          Anthropic describes Claude Mythos Preview identifying vulnerabilities
          and developing exploits, and presents Project Glasswing as an effort
          to give defenders an advantage. Its published examples include flaws
          it says were reported to maintainers and patched. These are the
          provider’s account of capabilities and outcomes.
        </p><p>
          <a
            href="https://www.anthropic.com/glasswing"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">Read Anthropic’s account ↗</a
          >
        </p><p>
          Finding a vulnerability, exploiting one, and introducing a new flaw in
          generated code are different behaviours. None establishes the others
          by itself.
        </p><p>
          Our inference: defensive access should be judged by protection
          delivered, including whether maintainers can validate and distribute
          repairs. More findings alone need not mean safer systems.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </details>
  <details class="evidence">
    <summary
      ><span>When should a finding become public?</span><DisclosureIcon
      /></summary
    ><div class="evidence-content">
      <div>
        <h3>Give repairs time.</h3><p>
          Confidential notification can give maintainers a window to patch. That
          window needs an owner, support, and a review date; indefinite secrecy
          leaves users unable to judge their exposure.
        </p>
      </div><div>
        <h3>Give users an account.</h3><p>
          A public advisory can help users respond and scrutinise the process.
          Publishing actionable exploit details too early may increase exposure.
          Separate urgent notification, mitigation advice, and later technical
          disclosure.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </details>
  <div class="chapter-takeaway">
    <p>
      Access should lead<br /><em>to protection.</em>
    </p><a href="#obligation-3">03 / Scrutinise who gets access ↗</a><br /><a
      href="#obligation-5">05 / Share findings responsibly ↗</a
    >
  </div>
</ChapterFrame>
